
ENGLISH 16

&
\begin{verbatim}


RC_Substitution1
( There also is a RC_Substitution2 (ENGLISH 15))


 1. RObjNPsubst (objrel, indobjrel, predrel/NP in VP, nongeneric )
 2. RSubjNPsubst (subjrel/NP in S , nongeneric )
 2b. RShiftNPsubst (shiftrel/NP in S , nongeneric )
 3a RPrepNPsubst (locargrel, dirargrel, complrel/NP in PREPP in VP, nongeneric)
 3b RVarPrepNPsubst (prepobjrel, byobjrel/NP in VARPREPP in VP, nongeneric)
 3c RVarPrepgensubst (byobjrel/generic in VARPREPP in VP)
 4a. RLocAdvSubst (locadvrel,sentadvrel/(VAR)PREPP, ADVP)
 4b. RShiftLocAdvSubst (shiftrel/loc,sentadv(VAR)PREPP, ADVP)
 5a. RSentAdvSubst (leftdislocrel/ADVP)
 5b. RSentPreppSubst (leftdislocrel/PREPP)
 6. RPROSentSubst (PROSENT )
 7. RSoPROSentSubst (soPROSENT )
 8. RNotProSentSubst (notPROSENT)
 9. RPosSubst (POS )
10. RSentNegSubst (NEG, no melting, in S)
11. RSentMeltNegSubst (NEG, melting, in S)
12. RVPMeltNegSubst (NEG, melting, in VP)
13a. RShiftPrepNPSubst (NP in shiftrel/PREPP in S)
13b. RShiftVarPrepNPSubst (NP in shiftrel/VARPREPP in S)

14. From Lisette: RTempAdvSubstitution1 and 2

Also to be solved: 
- tempadvrel, locadvrel/PREPPs which have a preposition that must be 
  deleted (He left early (on) that morning; cf. the substitution rules 
  in the NPsubgrammar, where relatives show the same behaviour: De dag 
  dat (i.p.v. waarop) hij weg ging.
- advs like `together', `-self' (He did it himself), `both', which 
  should be checked on their link with the antecedent.
- (VAR)PREPP in VERBP voor vpadvrel/instradvrel (see Dutch 4b; see 
  RPrepNPsubst; I hit him with a hammer)
- superdeixis for PREPPs; also try to prohibit the occurrence of
  prepps and advps that have no VARs as arguments (i.e. that have 
  already been substituted into, in thier own subgrammar)

\end{verbatim}


\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
%
% RULE RObjNPSubst    (* NP in VP *)
< m1: CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ mu1,
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                     [ mu2,
                       I2:rel1/I1::NPVAR{NPVARrec1},
                       mu3
                     ],
              mu4
            ]

  SUBST:
  m2: T3=I3::NP{NPrec1}[I5:mu5]
>

< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ mu1,
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                     [ mu2,
                       I7:rel1/T4=I4::NP{NPrec2}[I6:mu6],
                       mu3
                     ],
              mu4
            ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       m2: QUOTE_gsubstordercondition2(mu3,mu4,T3, CLAUSErec1.prosubject) AND
           NOT QUOTE_NegPhrase(T3)
       m : ALL
           QUOTE_substordercondition2(mu3,mu4, CLAUSErec1.prosubject)
       I1: NPVARrec1.index = LEVEL
       I2: rel1 IN [objrel, indobjrel, predrel]
       I3: NOT ( NPrec1.NPhead IN [reflNP, reciproNP,
                                   sentNP, openingNP] )
       I4: NOT ( NPrec2.NPhead IN [reflNP, reciproNP,
                                   sentNP, openingNP] )
       I5: NOT EXIST (mu5, [head/BIGPRO{}[mu10]])
       I6: NOT EXIST (mu6, [head/BIGPRO{}[mu11]])
       I7: rel1 IN [objrel, indobjrel, predrel] AND
           NOT QUOTE_NegPhrase(T4)
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: NPVARrec1 = COPYT_NPtoVAR(NPrec1) 
                                   EXCEPT FOR [cases] AND
              NPrec1.cases = [] AND
              NPrec1.generic = nogeneric
            C11: CLAUSErec1.deixis = omegadeixis AND
                 NPrec1.superdeixis = CLAUSErec1.superdeixis
            A11: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec1, mu5, NPrec2, mu6, NPVARrec1.cases)
            C12: CLAUSErec1.deixis <> omegadeixis AND
                 NPrec1.superdeixis = CLAUSErec1.deixis
            A12: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec1, mu5, NPrec2, mu6, NPVARrec1.cases)
          A1: NPrec2.superdeixis := omegadeixis;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: NPrec2.generic = omegageneric AND
              NPrec2.superdeixis = omegadeixis
            C11: CLAUSErec1.deixis = omegadeixis
            A11: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec2, mu6, NPrec1, mu5, []);
                 NPrec1.superdeixis := CLAUSErec1.superdeixis
            C12: CLAUSErec1.deixis <> omegadeixis
            A12: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec2, mu6, NPrec1, mu5, []);
                 NPrec1.superdeixis := CLAUSErec1.deixis
          A1: NPVARrec1 := COPYT_NPtoVAR(NPrec2);
              (* including cases *)
              NPVARrec1 := { index   : LEVEL
                             generic : nogeneric };
              NPrec1.generic := nogeneric
         >
&
\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RObjNPSubst
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
1. objrel: x1 see x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 see him;\\
\ \ \ objrel from ExistNpFormation: There was x1 $\rightarrow$ There was 
a doctor\\
2. indobjrel: x1 give x3 x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 give x3 a book $\rightarrow$ 
x1 give him a book\\
3. predrel from IdentNpFormation: This is x1 $\rightarrow$ This is John.
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_Substitution
\item[* date of creation:] 15-APR-1988 14:38:02  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch Rsubstitution1
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 15/03/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
1) added conditions on absence of BIGPROs (neeed for NOC)\\
2) added conditions on absence of an element with incorporated negation, since 
such a word must be broken down to a separate negation (no local translation of 
a word like `nobody' should be possible, except when translating bare NPs)
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To substitute nongeneric NPs for NPVARs under VP, in 
particular variables bearing relation objrel, predrel or indobjrel.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) the condition 
on non-genericity of NP1 is needed because for generic NPs more 
constraints on the model can be formulated. However, the surface parser does 
not know the difference, so that in surface all NPs are set to `omegageneric'.
Hence, NP2 is made omegageneric in the present rule.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
The function `assigncase' includes a COPYT\_ element, so that in analysis the 
link between NP1 and NPVAR1 (which should be the same) is laid via NP2.\\
Also, it is assumed that all CNVARs are in shiftrel now, and will be 
substituted for in the NPsubgrammar.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\
When surface orders of two languages with respect to quantificational variables 
differ, no translation is possible at the moment: in analysis of the sentence 
{\em ..dat x1 aan iemand een boek gaf\/}, the leftmost argument is 
desubstituted first ({\em iemand\/}), and then the next one ({\em a book\/}). 
In generation, therefore, {\em a book\/} will be substituted first, to produce 
{\em x1 gave a book to x3\/}, but this is forbidden since there still is a 
VAR to the right (of course, in this case there is still 
another verbpattern which does yield the correct result, viz.\ {\em x1 give x3 
x2\/}, but this will not always happen).\\
The solution is to relax the substorder conditions somewhat in generation: the 
VAR occurring to the right may not be quantificational (scope-sensitive), but 
otherwise a VAR is allowed. When this does not help (as in the present example) 
substitution is still allowed even if there is a quantificational VAR, but an 
incorrectness bonus is given.
\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
Still to be added: polarity requirements

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}


\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RSubjNPsubst                       (* subj NP in S *)
< m1: CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ mu1,
              subjrel/I1::NPVAR{NPVARrec1},
              mu2,
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[mu3],
              mu4
            ] 

  SUBST:
  m2: T3=I3::NP{NPrec1}[I5:mu5]
>

< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ mu1,
              I7:subjrel/T4=I4::NP{NPrec2}[I6:mu6],
              mu2,
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[mu3],
              mu4
            ] 
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       m1: ALL
           CLAUSErec1.PROsubject = false AND
           ( NOT EXIST (mu2, [auxrel/I8::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu8]]) -> 
             ( [CLAUSErec1.thetavp] * [vp010, vp012] = [] OR
               CLAUSErec1.synvpefs * AUX_RaisingVps = [] ) 
           ) AND
           ( EXIST (mu2, [I9:auxrel/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu8]]) ->
             ( [VERBrec1.thetavp] * [vp010, vp012] = [] OR
               VERBrec1.synvps * AUX_RaisingVps = [] )
           )
       m2: ALL
           QUOTE_gsubstordercondition(mu2, mu3, mu4, T3, CLAUSErec1.prosubject) AND
           NOT QUOTE_NegPhrase(T3)
       m : ALL
           QUOTE_substordercondition(mu2, mu3, mu4, CLAUSErec1.prosubject) AND
           CLAUSErec1.PROsubject = false AND
           ( NOT EXIST (mu2, [auxrel/I8::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu8]]) -> 
             ( [CLAUSErec1.thetavp] * [vp010, vp012] = [] OR
               CLAUSErec1.synvpefs * AUX_RaisingVps = [] ) 
           ) AND
           ( EXIST (mu2, [auxrel/I8::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu8]]) ->
             ( [VERBrec1.thetavp] * [vp010, vp012] = [] OR
               VERBrec1.synvps * AUX_RaisingVps = [] )
           )
       I1: NPVARrec1.index = LEVEL
       I3: NOT ( NPrec1.NPhead IN [reflNP, reciproNP,
                                   sentNP, openingNP] )
       I4: NOT ( NPrec2.NPhead IN [reflNP, reciproNP,
                                   sentNP, openingNP] )
       I5: NOT EXIST (mu5, [head/BIGPRO{}[mu10]])
       I6: NOT EXIST (mu6, [head/BIGPRO{}[mu11]])
       I7: NOT QUOTE_NegPhrase(T4)
       I8: VERBrec1.subc = modalverb
       I9: VERBrec1.subc = modalverb AND
           NOT EXIST (mu8, [head/SUBVERB{SUBVERBrec1}
                                [head/BVERB(KEY_willBVERBkey){BVERBrec1}]])
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: NPVARrec1 = COPYT_NPtoVAR(NPrec1) 
                                   EXCEPT FOR [cases] AND
              NPrec1.cases = [] 
            C11: CLAUSErec1.deixis = omegadeixis AND
                 NPrec1.superdeixis = CLAUSErec1.superdeixis
                 C111: NPrec1.generic = nogeneric AND
                       [NPrec1.NPhead] * [oneNP, theyNP] = []
                 A111: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec1, mu5, NPrec2, mu6, NPVARrec1.cases)
                 C112: NPrec1.generic = yesgeneric AND
                       [NPrec1.NPhead] * [oneNP, theyNP] <> []
                 A112: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec1, mu5, NPrec2, mu6, NPVARrec1.cases)
            A11: @
            C12: CLAUSErec1.deixis <> omegadeixis AND
                 NPrec1.superdeixis = CLAUSErec1.deixis
                 C121: NPrec1.generic = nogeneric AND
                       [NPrec1.NPhead] * [oneNP, theyNP] = []
                 A121: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec1, mu5, NPrec2, mu6, NPVARrec1.cases)
                 C122: NPrec1.generic = yesgeneric AND
                       [NPrec1.NPhead] * [oneNP, theyNP] <> []
                 A122: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec1, mu5, NPrec2, mu6, NPVARrec1.cases)
            A12: @
          A1: NPrec2.superdeixis := omegadeixis;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: NPrec2.generic = omegageneric AND
              NPrec2.superdeixis = omegadeixis
            C11: CLAUSErec1.deixis = omegadeixis
                 C111: [NPrec2.NPhead] * [theyNP, oneNP] = []
                 A111: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec2, mu6, NPrec1, mu5, []);
                       NPVARrec1 := COPYT_NPtoVAR(NPrec2);
                         (* including cases *)
                       NPVARrec1 := { index   : LEVEL
                                      generic : nogeneric };
                       NPrec1.generic := nogeneric
                C112: [NPrec2.NPhead] * [theyNP, oneNP] <> []
                A112: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec2, mu6, NPrec1, mu5, []);
                      NPVARrec1 := COPYT_NPtoVAR(NPrec2);
                         (* including cases *)
                      NPVARrec1 := { index   : LEVEL
                                     generic : yesgeneric };
                      NPrec1.generic := yesgeneric
            A11: NPrec1.superdeixis := CLAUSErec1.superdeixis
            C12: CLAUSErec1.deixis <> omegadeixis
                 C121: [NPrec2.NPhead] * [theyNP, oneNP] = []
                 A121: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec2, mu6, NPrec1, mu5, []);
                       NPVARrec1 := COPYT_NPtoVAR(NPrec2);
                         (* including cases *)
                       NPVARrec1 := { index   : LEVEL
                                      generic : nogeneric };
                       NPrec1.generic := nogeneric
                C122: [NPrec2.NPhead] * [theyNP, oneNP] <> []
                A122: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec2, mu6, NPrec1, mu5, []);
                      NPVARrec1 := COPYT_NPtoVAR(NPrec2);
                        (* including cases *)
                      NPVARrec1 := { index   : LEVEL
                                     generic : yesgeneric };
                      NPrec1.generic := yesgeneric
            A12: NPrec1.superdeixis := CLAUSErec1.deixis
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RSubjNPsubst
\item[* relevant examples:] x1 do see him $\rightarrow$ John do see him;
when x1 do see him $\rightarrow$ when who did see him (when did who see him)
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_Substitution
\item[* date of creation:] 15-APR-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch RSubstitution2
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 15/03/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
1) added conditions on absence of BIGPROs (needed for NOC)\\
2) added conditions on absence of an element with incorporated negation, since 
such a word must be broken down to a separate negation (no local translation of 
a word like `nobody' should be possible, except when translating bare NPs)\\
3) added restrictions on possible vps of CLAUSE, to prevent unnecessary false 
paths (ergative verbs taking a closed prop (=modals) should substitute the 
subject only in the embedded clause, not also in the main clause)\\
4) added the condition ALL for idioms (ask Andre why...)
\item date: 14/08/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Added condition on thetavp/synvps of modalverb (iso CLAUSE) if present, to 
prevent subst. for 010 modal in main clause (should be done in embedded clause).
\item date: 20/09/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
split up original rule in two rules, one 
for subject substitution (the current one), and one for shifted NP substitution 
(RShiftNPSubst).
\item date: 10/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
relaxed conditions on substitution for modal `will' in generation: substitution 
is possible if demanded by source language, i.e.\ if in source language there 
was no `zullen010': {\em hij komt morgen $\rightarrow$ he {\em will} come 
tomorrow\/}. In analysis, this will only translate in {\em hij zal morgen 
komen\/}.
\item date: 17/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo\\
added condition/actions for generic theyNP and oneNP
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To substitute NPs for NPVARs under S, in particular for
variables bearing relation subjrel
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
