\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RSentAdvSubst
\item[* relevant examples:] x1 do smoke + probably $\rightarrow$
probably, x1 do smoke\\ (He probably smokes, the man whom I probably saw smoking)
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_Substitution
\item[* date of creation:] 19-APR-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch RSubstitution9
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 15/03/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
added conditions on absence of an element with incorporated negation, since 
such a word must be broken down to a separate negation (no local translation of 
a word like `nowhere' should be possible, except when translating bare NPs). It 
is doubtful whether sentadvs with incorporated negation exist at all.
\item date: 8-OCT-1988\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted to substitute for ADVPs instead of OPENADVPPROPs
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To insert sentential adverbs into the sentence, in a 
leftdislocrel position, separated from the rest of the sentence by a comma (
which probably should be optional in analysis). No hoprules or alternative 
sentadvvar-insertion rules have been written yet, so that the attribute 
`position' is not used yet, and the remarks below not really pertain to the 
case dealt with in this rule.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) Sentential 
adverbs are dealt with in a specific rule, since they behave different from 
other adverbs:\\
1. They cannot occur after NOT: $^{*}$The man has not probably been drinking, 
etc. (vs. He has not been working hard)\\
2. They cannot be questioned: $^{*}$How probably has the man been 
drinking? (vs. How hard has he been working)\\
3. They cannot be moved out of their own sentence: $^{*}$How probably do you 
think that 
the man has been drinking (vs. How hard do you think that he has been working)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
Some kind of hop transformation is needed to account for other surface 
positions of sentential adverbs, e.g.\ `The man probably has been drinking' 
and `The man has probably been drinking'. Perhaps the position attribute for 
adverbs will be needed there too. Jan O. has suggested to have the adverbial 
hop-rules work before this rule-class, and have a number of SentAdvSubst rules 
here, one for each hop-position.
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

