\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RSoPROsentSubst
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
x1 think x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 think so (verb of belief)\\
x1 tell x3 x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 tell x3 so (verb of saying)
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_Substitution
\item[* date of creation:] 19-APR-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] RPROsentSubst
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To substitute soPROSENTs for PROSENTVARs
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
For the moment it is assumed that all verbs taking a SoProSent take it in the 
same way, although there seems ample reason to distinguish between verbs 
preferring the `So' in inital position and verbs taking it where it is 
postulated in the current rule (usually VP-finally): I think so - So I 
understand. Perhaps this difference is related to two different meanings of 
`So': the final `so' being the ordinary ProSent, the initial `so' being more 
like a `thus'. However, the translation of this `thus' would probably still be 
something like a preposed `Dat' in Dutch, like any SoProSent, so perhaps 
no difference in meaning is involved at all.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\
This rule is to be mapped onto a Dutch rule with preposed `Dat' + inversion for 
other verbs than verbs of belief (esp.\ verbs of saying: I told you so 
$\rightarrow$ Dat zei ik je/Ik zei het je, see RProSentSubst), and onto a Dutch 
rule with `van wel' for verbs of belief (I hope so $\rightarrow$ Ik hoop van 
wel). Negation of the SoProsent for verbs other than of belief is 
straightforward in Dutch (I didn't say so $\rightarrow$ Dat zei ik niet), but 
for verbs of belief there is a problem in Dutch: I don't think so $\rightarrow$
$^{*}$Ik denk niet van wel. To solve the problem by simply mapping all English 
SoProSents on a Dutch version with `Dat' or `het' is too simple: `I believe so' 
can hardly be translated as `Dat geloof ik'.
\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

