\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RPosSubst
\item[* relevant examples:] he does POS go, he probably has POS given it away,
(I always thought) that you did POS go there for fun
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_Substitution
\item[* date of creation:] 28-APR-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch RSubstitution11
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To substitute the element POS for a POSVAR in the sentence. In 
the DO-
deletion rules, DO wil only be deleted if it is adjacent to the following verb. 
If POS intervenes, DO will not be deleted, but POS will.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) It is assumed 
that a POS can be substituted everywhere, although of course it has no
function in a sentence without the auxiliary `do'. Restrictions exist on the 
occurrence of negrels in the model (see RSentPosVar).
Deletion of POS in infinite sentences and sentences withou the auxiliary `do' 
will have to be arranged together with 
do-deletion, in the DO-deletion Rules ($^{*}$It would be foolish to do go there 
(Het zou dom zijn om er wel heen te 
gaan). Or perhaps a paraphrase can be found? (It would be foolish if one would 
go there...)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
In analysis, a POS will never be inserted unless there is 
an `extra' DO, i.e.\ a sentence like `He could come' will not be made 
ambiguous between `Hij kon komen' and `Hij kon wel komen'.\\
The condition on the absence of a negrel in the VERBP (see RSentPosVar) is 
given because it is 
assumed that sentences like `He did give nobody a kiss' are wrong. If 
acceptable examples can be found, this condition should perhaps be relaxed 
somewhat. (? I did hear nothing).
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
